**Personal Independence Payment (PIP)** helps with the extra costs of living with a long-term physical or mental health condition or disability. You do not have to spend it on anything in particular.

## Who can get PIP?

You may be able to get PIP if **all** of these apply:

- you are **16 or over** and **under State Pension age**
- you have a health condition or disability that makes it hard to do **everyday tasks** or **get around**
- you have had these difficulties for at least **3 months** and expect them to last at least **9 more months**
- you live in **England or Wales** and have lived in Great Britain for at least 2 of the last 3 years (there are some exceptions)

You do **not** need to have paid National Insurance, and it does not matter whether you work.

If you are **terminally ill** and may have 12 months or less to live, there are **special rules** that make it faster and easier to claim.

{% callout type="info" title="Living in Scotland?" %}
In Scotland, PIP has been replaced by **Adult Disability Payment**, paid by Social Security Scotland.
{% /callout %}

## The two parts of PIP

PIP has two parts. You can get one, or both.

- **Daily living part:** for help with things like preparing food, washing, dressing, managing medicines, talking to people and managing money.
- **Mobility part:** for help with planning a journey and moving around.

Each part is paid at a **standard** or an **enhanced** rate. The rates are shown above.

## How PIP is decided: points

PIP uses a points system. The DWP looks at **12 activities**: 10 for daily living and 2 for mobility. For each activity, it picks the statement that best describes you, and each statement is worth between 0 and 12 points.

| Points in a part | What you get for that part |
| --- | --- |
| 0 to 7 | Nothing for that part |
| 8 to 11 | Standard rate |
| 12 or more | Enhanced rate |

Read [the 12 PIP activities explained](/benefits/pip/activities/), or try our free self-check to see which statements may apply to you.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## Two rules that make a big difference

**Most days.** A statement applies if it is true on **more than half of days**. If your condition changes a lot, think about the whole year, not just good days.

**Reliably.** You only count as able to do something if you can do it **safely, to a good enough standard, as often as you need to, and in a reasonable time**. If a task causes a lot of pain, takes you much longer, or wipes you out afterwards, that counts.

## How to claim

1. **Start your claim** by phone on 0800 917 2222, or online at GOV.UK.
2. **Fill in the "How your disability affects you" form**, which the DWP will send you.
3. **Have an assessment** with a health professional, usually by phone, video or face to face.
4. **Get a decision** letter from the DWP.

See our step by step guide on [how to claim PIP](/benefits/pip/how-to-claim/), or download our free [guide to filling in the PIP form (PDF)](/guides/how-to-fill-in-your-pip-form.pdf).

## What PIP can lead to

Getting PIP can open the door to other help, such as:

- a **Blue Badge** for parking
- the **Motability Scheme**, if you get the enhanced mobility rate
- **Carer's Allowance** for someone who cares for you
- extra money in some **means-tested benefits**, such as Pension Credit or Housing Benefit
- a **Disabled Persons Railcard**, and possibly a free bus pass from your council
- not being affected by the **benefit cap**

## If you disagree with a decision

Many decisions are changed when people challenge them. You have **one month** from the date on your decision letter to ask the DWP to look again. Read [how to challenge a PIP decision](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/).
